    <body>
      <sec id="article-27801.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Prolactinoma is a prolactin-secreting pituitary adenoma and the most common functional pituitary tumor. Excess prolactin leads to hyperprolactinemia, which may cause menstrual irregularities, infertility, and galactorrhea in women and decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, and hypogonadism in men. Tumor enlargement, particularly in macroadenomas, can produce mass effect, resulting in headache, visual field defects, and cranial nerve palsies.&#x000a0;Evaluation begins with measurement of serum prolactin levels and exclusion of secondary causes, including pregnancy, hypothyroidism, and medication-induced hyperprolactinemia. Pituitary magnetic resonance imaging with gadolinium is used to define tumor size and assess proximity to the optic chiasm. Formal visual field testing is indicated when chiasmal compression is suspected. Dopamine agonists are first-line therapy and commonly normalize prolactin levels while reducing tumor size. Surgery and radiotherapy are reserved for medication intolerance, resistance, or selected clinical circumstances.</p>
        <p>This activity reviews evidence-based approaches to initiating and titrating dopamine agonists, monitoring biochemical and radiographic response, and identifying indications for surgical or radiotherapy referral. This activity also focuses on interpreting prolactin levels in a clinical context, recognizing assay limitations such as the hook effect, and distinguishing prolactinoma from other causes of hyperprolactinemia.&#x000a0;This activity equips healthcare providers with the knowledge and skills to counsel patients effectively on pregnancy planning, symptom monitoring, and long-term follow-up to reduce the risk of recurrence.&#x000a0;In addition, this activity emphasizes the importance of interprofessional collaboration among healthcare providers, highlighting coordinated counseling on pregnancy planning, structured long-term follow-up, and strategies to reduce recurrence risk, thereby ensuring comprehensive and integrated care to improve patient outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the clinical features and biochemical findings suggestive of prolactinoma in patients presenting with hyperprolactinemia.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select appropriate first-line therapy, including dopamine agonists, based on tumor characteristics and patient-specific factors.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based protocols for titrating dopamine agonists and monitoring response with serial prolactin measurements and pituitary magnetic resonance imaging.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate&#x000a0;with multidisciplinary healthcare teams, including endocrinology, neurosurgery, radiology, ophthalmology, pharmacy, and nursing, to optimize treatment adherence, interdisciplinary care, and patient outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=27801&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=27801">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Prolactin-secreting tumors of the pituitary gland are called prolactinomas. They are the most common secretory tumors of the pituitary gland, accounting for up to 40% of all pituitary adenomas (see <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Prolactinoma).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r1">[1]</xref> Prolactinomas cause a wide variety of symptoms related either to the mass effect of the tumor or to prolactin hypersecretion. Based on the size of the tumor, prolactinomas can be classified as microprolactinomas (&#x0003c;10 mm), macroprolactinomas (&#x0003e;10 mm), or giant prolactinomas (&#x0003e;4 cm).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r2">[2]</xref> Hyperprolactinemia is not specific to prolactinoma, and alternative causes such as pregnancy, medications, hypothyroidism, and pituitary stalk effect due to other pituitary tumors should be considered in the differential diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The exact cause of prolactinomas remains poorly understood. Prolactinomas often arise from the monoclonal expansion of pituitary lactotrophs that have undergone somatic mutations. Overexpression of the pituitary tumor&#x02013;transforming gene&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">(PTTG</italic>) and&#x000a0;mutations in the receptor for fibroblast growth factor 4&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">(FGF4)</italic>&#x000a0;have been identified in pituitary adenomas, particularly prolactinomas. Most prolactinomas are sporadic, but they can also occur as part of familial syndromes. Prolactinoma can be&#x000a0;part of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">(MEN1</italic>); approximately 15% to 60% of patients with&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">MEN1</italic>&#x000a0;develop a pituitary adenoma, and the majority&#x000a0;are prolactinomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Prolactinomas account for approximately 40% to 50% of all clinically recognized pituitary adenomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r1">[1]</xref> The mean prevalence of prolactinomas is estimated to be approximately 10 per 100,000 in men and 30 per 100,000 in women, with a peak prevalence in women aged 25 to 44. Among patients with prolactinomas, 60% of men present with macroprolactinomas, whereas 90% of women present with microprolactinomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Prolactinomas arise from the monoclonal expansion of pituitary lactotrophs and are mostly benign, often sharply demarcated without evidence of invasion. Some&#x000a0;prolactinomas may behave aggressively, including invasion of surrounding structures, and typically exhibit increased mitotic activity, higher cellularity, and greater pleomorphism. The diagnosis of malignant prolactinoma requires the presence of distant extracranial involvement. The lateral portions of the anterior pituitary are the most common sites involved in prolactinomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Rarely, mixed pituitary tumors secreting prolactin in combination with growth hormone, adrenocorticotropic hormone, or thyroid-stimulating hormone may also be seen, which are recognized by immunohistochemistry.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Microadenomas (&#x0003c;1 cm) are usually confined to the sella turcica and do not cause compressive symptoms, whereas macroadenomas (&#x0003e;1 cm) can expand to an adjacent structure, such as the optic chiasm and cavernous sinuses, and cause compressive symptoms, such as visual field defects, cranial nerve palsy, and headaches. Elevated prolactin levels mainly cause symptoms of microadenomas.</p>
        <p>Prolactin levels are usually directly proportional to tumor size, with levels of approximately 200 ng/mL in tumors less than 1 cm, 200 to 1000 ng/mL in tumors measuring 1 to 2 cm, and greater than 1000 ng/mL in tumors larger than 2 cm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r14">[14]</xref> If prolactin levels do not match the tumor size, this mismatch may reflect a poorly differentiated prolactinoma or the presence of a large cystic&#x000a0;tumor component.</p>
        <p>The hypothalamus exerts a predominant inhibitory influence on prolactin secretion via dopamine, and any factor that disrupts this mechanism leads to hyperprolactinemia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r15">[15]</xref> Given that increased prolactin secretion is observed in many physiological and pathological states, including those unrelated to prolactinomas, it is essential to consider the various causes of hyperprolactinemia.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Prolactinomas clinically present&#x000a0;due to&#x000a0;tumor mass effect or hyperprolactinemia. Microprolactinomas (&#x0003c;1 cm) can present with symptoms&#x000a0;related to hyperprolactinemia or&#x000a0;may be detected incidentally on neuroimaging performed for other reasons. In contrast, macroprolactinomas more commonly present with mass effects on the surrounding structures.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Signs and Symptoms Due to Mass Effect</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Headaches.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Changes in vision: Visual field deficits, blurred vision, and decreased visual acuity.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cranial nerve palsy:&#x000a0;Particularly in invasive tumors or pituitary apoplexy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Seizures, hydrocephalus, and unilateral exophthalmos:&#x000a0;These are rare presentations.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pituitary apoplexy: A&#x000a0;medical emergency caused by a spontaneous hemorrhage into the pituitary tumor, presenting with severe headaches, vision changes, and panhypopituitarism.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Signs and Symptoms Due to Hyperprolactinemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Men</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Decreased libido</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Impotence</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Erectile dysfunction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Oilgozoospermia due to secondary hypogonadism</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Women</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infertility</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Loss of libido</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Galactorrhea</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Children and adolescents</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Growth arrest</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pubertal delay</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Primary amenorrhea</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Additional features, such as osteopenia, anxiety, depression, fatigue, and emotional instability, were observed in both sexes. Approximately 10% of prolactinomas can also secrete growth hormone; thus, gigantism and acromegaly occur in these patients.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>A comprehensive history and physical examination are&#x000a0;essential&#x000a0;to exclude alternative causes of hyperprolactinemia and to document any visual field deficits, galactorrhea, growth changes, hypopituitarism, menstrual irregularities, impotence, or infertility. Formal visual field testing by an ophthalmologist is recommended, especially in patients with macroadenomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The differential diagnoses to consider during evaluation are listed below.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Physiological Causes</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pregnancy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Nipple stimulation in lactating&#x000a0;women</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Stress</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Pathological Causes</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hypothalamic disorders</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Craniopharyngiomas, meningioma, and dysgerminoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Nonsecreting pituitary adenoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sarcoidosis and histiocytosis X</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cranial irradiation</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>&#x000a0;Pituitary disorders</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Prolactinoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Acromegaly and Cushing disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Empty sella syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lymphocytic hypophysitis&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Medications (dopamine antagonists)</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Antipsychotics</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tricyclic antidepressants and selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Antiemetic agents, including metoclopramide, domperidone, and prochlorperazine</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Antihypertensives such as verapamil and &#x003b1;-methyldopa</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Opioid analgesics, including morphine and methadone</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypothyroidism</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Idiopathic hyperprolactinemia</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Laboratory Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>Laboratory evaluation begins with the measurement of serum prolactin levels. If prolactin level is high, additional testing should include a comprehensive metabolic panel, thyroid-stimulating hormone, and a pregnancy test for women of childbearing age. Evaluation of other pituitary hormones should be guided by the patient&#x02019;s age and sex and may include cortisol, adrenocorticotropic hormone, insulin-like growth factor 1, luteinizing hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone, and testosterone or estradiol. This broader assessment helps identify hypopituitarism or the presence of co-secreting pituitary tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients may have markedly elevated prolactin levels; however, assay results can be falsely low due to the "hook effect".<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Serial&#x000a0;dilution of the serum sample with repeat measurement of prolactin levels is helpful when clinical suspicion remains high. Although the hook effect is uncommon with modern assays, it should be considered when prolactin levels are only mildly elevated despite a high clinical suspicion for a macroprolactinoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Another condition in which measured prolactin may be elevated despite normal biologically active prolactin levels is the presence of high&#x02013;molecular-weight prolactin, known as macroprolactin. Screening for macroprolactin should be considered in patients with asymptomatic hyperprolactinemia. Pretreatment of the serum with polyethylene glycol can precipitate macroprolactin prior to performing the prolactin immunoassay.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Imaging</bold>
</p>
        <p>A&#x000a0;computed tomography scan may demonstrate the mass, but magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) with gadolinium is the preferred imaging modality for evaluating hyperprolactinemia, as it best depicts the anatomy of the hypothalamic-pituitary region.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients with tumors adjacent to or compressing the optic chiasm should be referred for formal visual field testing.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Treatment and Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>Incidentally discovered microprolactinomas that are asymptomatic may be managed with observation and periodic monitoring of laboratory values and imaging studies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;Dopamine agonist therapy should be used to treat macrolactinoma or symptomatic microadenomas. Treatment goals include tumor shrinkage, visual field restoration when&#x000a0;a defect is present, reversal of galactorrhea, and restoration of fertility or abnormal sexual function. Cabergoline is the preferred agent due to its higher frequency of normalizing prolactin levels and inducing tumor shrinkage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Most prolactinomas are successfully treated with medical therapy, while surgery and radiotherapy are reserved for refractory disease.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Medical Therapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Medical therapy is the preferred treatment for prolactinomas, in contrast to most other functional pituitary tumors. Cabergoline and bromocriptine are the most commonly used dopamine agonists. Pergolide has been withdrawn from the market because of its association with valvular heart disease, and quinagolide is not available in the United States.</p>
        <p>Dopamine agonists suppress prolactin synthesis and secretion and inhibit lactotroph proliferation, leading to tumor shrinkage. Cabergoline is generally preferred because it more frequently normalizes prolactin levels and tumor shrinkage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Cabergoline also has a more favorable adverse effect profile than bromocriptine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Common adverse effects include gastrointestinal symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, abdominal discomfort, and dizziness. Gastrointestinal adverse effects can be reduced by administering the medication with food, and taking it at dinner or at night to minimize vertigo or sleepiness. Rarely, a patient can develop impulse control disorders due to the dopamine agonistic action of these medications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Cabergoline: Cabergoline is typically initiated at a low dose of 0.25 mg twice weekly or 0.5 mg once weekly to improve tolerability. Prolactin levels should be reassessed after 1 to 3 months; if the levels normalize, the current dose can be continued. If the levels improve but remain unnormalized, the dosage can be gradually increased to a maximum of 2 mg weekly. When doses exceeding 2 mg per week are required, cardiac echocardiography should be performed every 2 to 3 years because of the potential increased risk of valvular heart disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r31">[31]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Bromocriptine:&#x000a0;Bromocriptine is not considered a first-line treatment option due to the higher risk of adverse effects and lower efficacy. Approximately 25% of patients&#x000a0;are resistant to bromocriptine, compared with about 10% to cabergoline.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;Treatment with bromocriptine is&#x000a0;started at 1.25 or 2.5 mg taken after dinner or at bedtime, daily for 1 week, then increased in increments by 2.5 mg/d as tolerated, up to a usual maximum dose of 15 mg/d. If the response is inadequate, switching to cabergoline should be strongly considered.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Treatment with dopamine agonists for at least 2 years is generally recommended to reduce the risk of recurrence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;Normalization of prolactin levels and a reduction in tumor volume of more than 25% after 3 months of cabergoline therapy are significant predictors of long-term response.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;Approximately&#x000a0;20% of patients remain in remission after discontinuing cabergoline. Predictors of successful withdrawal from these medications include the use of low maintenance doses of cabergoline, treatment duration longer than 2 years, and a substantial reduction in adenoma size.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>If dopamine agonist therapy withdrawal is attempted after at least 2 years of therapy, serum prolactin levels should be measured every 3 months in the first year and annually thereafter. Estrogen plus progesterone replacement therapy&#x000a0;may be used to manage hypoestrogenism in women with an inadequate response to treatment who do not desire fertility. Women who desire pregnancy&#x000a0;may need ovulation inducers. For men with inadequate treatment response, testosterone therapy (if no fertility is desired) or human chorionic gonadotropin (if fertility is desired) should be initiated.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgery</bold>
</p>
        <p>Transsphenoidal surgery is the preferred surgical option for the following reasons:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Failure of medical therapy to adequately reduce prolactin levels or tumor size despite several months of maximally tolerated dose or intolerance to dopamine agonists.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Women&#x000a0;with large prolactinomas (&#x0003e;3 cm) who desire&#x000a0;pregnancy, given the risk of tumor enlargement during pregnancy.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Radiation therapy should be considered for residual tumors after surgical procedures or in cases resistant to cabergoline therapy.</p>
        <p>Some study results demonstrate better outcomes, higher remission rates, and higher&#x000a0;health-related quality-of-life scores, even in patients with microprolactinemia undergoing surgical resection rather than long-term dopamine agonist therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Pregnancy</bold>
</p>
        <p>In women with prolactinomas measuring 2 to 3 cm or smaller, cabergoline is recommended due to its better efficacy and safety during pregnancy. However, safety data for cabergoline are less robust than those for bromocriptine. In women with large, symptomatic adenomas, tumor debulking or removal may be considered before conception.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r40">[40]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Menopause</bold>
</p>
        <p>After menopause, management of hypoestrogenemia is usually unnecessary,&#x000a0;and dopamine agonist therapy may be discontinued, allowing prolactin levels to rise. Prolactin levels should be monitored periodically. Pituitary MRI is indicated if prolactin levels increase substantially, particularly above 200 ng/mL. In patients with macroadenomas, therapy should be resumed if there is significant growth in adenoma size.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r42">[42]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The following conditions are included in the differential diagnosis of prolactinoma:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pregnancy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypothyroidism</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Renal failure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Breast stimulation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pituitary tumors</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Approximately&#x000a0;20% of patients remain in remission after discontinuing cabergoline. Predictors of successful withdrawal from these medications include the need for low-maintenance doses of cabergoline, a treatment duration of more than 2 years, and a substantial reduction in adenoma size.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r36">[36]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>The following are the potential complications of prolactinoma:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Mass effect leading to visual deficits, cranial nerve palsy, or pituitary apoplexy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infertility</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Osteoporosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgery-related complications, including loss of vision, cerebrospinal fluid leak, and permanent hypopituitarism</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Seizures</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s12" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>The following consultations are helpful in the treatment of prolactinomas:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Neurosurgeon</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Radiation oncologist</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Endocrinologist</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Prolactin is a hormone secreted by the pituitary gland. A prolactinoma is a pituitary adenoma that results in elevated blood prolactin levels due to excessive hormone production. Common symptoms include&#x000a0;menstrual irregularities in women, low testosterone in men, breast milk secretion, weak bones, headache, and visual field defects. MRI of the&#x000a0;pituitary is required for diagnosis and tumor evaluation.</p>
        <p>In the vast majority of cases, prolactinomas can be effectively managed with medical therapy alone, which normalizes prolactin levels and reduces tumor size. Cabergoline is the preferred therapy due to its higher efficacy and better tolerability. In a minority of cases, medical therapy is ineffective, and surgical resection or debulking should be considered. Surgery may also be considered in selected cases, such as women with tumors larger than 3 cm who desire pregnancy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>During pregnancy, hyperplasia of pituitary lactotrophs occurs, resulting in increased prolactin levels. Prolactinomas may enlarge during pregnancy, particularly macroadenomas. Surgical intervention may be considered prior to conception in patients with known macroadenomas. Patients receiving medical therapy who become pregnant should be monitored closely, including periodic visual field assessment when indicated. Bromocriptine has the most extensive safety data in pregnancy and is the preferred agent when treatment is required.</p>
        <p>Pituitary apoplexy is an endocrine emergency resulting from a spontaneous hemorrhage or prolactinoma. Patients typically present with a sudden-onset severe headache and visual disturbances. Individuals with a known prolactinoma or other pituitary adenoma who develop these symptoms require urgent MRI as well as neurosurgical and endocrine evaluation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Following the diagnosis of a prolactinoma, comprehensive patient education is essential to reduce morbidity and improve long-term outcomes. Patients should receive clear counseling regarding the manifestations of prolactinoma and the importance of seeking medical attention for new or worsening symptoms. When tapering or discontinuing medical therapy is considered, periodic imaging is required to monitor for tumor recurrence or regrowth. Pharmacists play a key role in educating patients about dopamine agonist therapy, including proper administration and potential adverse effects. Nursing staff should reinforce education regarding treatment expectations and, when appropriate, discuss the potential role of radiation therapy for large or refractory tumors and the associated risk of hypopituitarism.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Outcomes</bold>
</p>
        <p>The prognosis for patients with microprolactinomas is generally excellent, and long-term medical therapy is often effective. In contrast, macroprolactinomas may enlarge over time and sometimes require more aggressive management. Because the growth pattern of macroprolactinomas is variable and unpredictable, careful monitoring of tumor size is necessary. Decisions regarding tapering or discontinuing medical therapy should be made cautiously, as tumor regrowth can occur after treatment withdrawal.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27801.r45">[45]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27801.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
